<urn:usf:contract:provider-capability-universe:1> <urn:usf:predicate:nonClaim> "A shared vocabulary is not a shared claim. A model feature stays an observation of catalogue metadata and a task class stays an earned qualification; joining them on one route must never let either borrow the other's evidence." <urn:usf:graph:provider-capability-universe> .
<urn:usf:contract:provider-capability-universe:1> <urn:usf:predicate:nonClaim> "Reporting the universe is not widening it. This contract changes which routes are visible and how they are described, not which routes an operator has authorised or may be billed for." <urn:usf:graph:provider-capability-universe> .
<urn:usf:contract:provider-capability-universe:1> <urn:usf:predicate:nonClaim> "The write set is widened by exactly the paths the routing-assurance repairs touch: the economics evaluator, its graph projection, the host-CLI worker turn, the four unit test files whose fixtures carry an authorization shape, and the derived economics projection itself. Widening a contract normally stales its cohort; this contract has exactly one work, that work held no current construction grant, and the cost was measured before the change rather than assumed." <urn:usf:graph:provider-capability-universe> .
<urn:usf:contract:provider-capability-universe:1> <urn:usf:predicate:nonClaim> "config/execution-economics.json is writable through both mechanisms and that is deliberate, not ambiguity. It is a derived projection: usf transact regenerates it from graph authority through the tracked reader, and this work writes it when the reader's own output shape changes, which no transaction can do because the transaction runs the reader it is replacing. Both writers must produce the same bytes from the same graph, and the projection's own verification is what proves they did." <urn:usf:graph:provider-capability-universe> .
<urn:usf:contract:provider-capability-universe:1> <urn:usf:predicate:nonClaim> "src/commands/hermes.ts is not in this write set. Hermes reaches the universe through graph-execute, which is, so the supervisor needs no edit to inherit the shared vocabulary." <urn:usf:graph:provider-capability-universe> .
<urn:usf:contract:provider-capability-universe:1> <urn:usf:predicate:objective> "Derive every provider route projection from one classified universe, and give a capability one meaning across it, so that what the operator is told about a route matches what execution will actually select, and a capability a work unit requires is satisfied by evidence rather than silently ignored." <urn:usf:graph:provider-capability-universe> .
<urn:usf:contract:provider-capability-universe:1> <urn:usf:predicate:objectiveEvidence> "Measured on 2026-09-04 against a clean tree at 7de1938. Three surfaces disagreed about the same repository: usf status reported 2 configured routes from config/providers.json, usf providers census reported 12 candidates of three kinds, and usf execute classified 7525 catalogue models into 136 ROUTABLE and 4 AVAILABLE and then answered from cohere north-mini-code-1-0 in 879 ms at zero price. Two capability vocabularies existed with zero overlap: work packets declare modelCapability across nine task classes led by typescript at 22 and node-test at 20, while the catalogue carries only attachment, reasoning, structured-output, temperature and tool-call. graph-execute read urn:usf:predicate:requiredCapability, of which the graph holds zero instances, so no capability requirement has ever constrained a dispatch." <urn:usf:graph:provider-capability-universe> .
